{
  "abstract": "For the shortcut Collatz map, let $h(x)$ be the least peak of two forward paths, one from $x$ and one from a smaller positive integer, ending at the same state. Both clocks are free, including zero. We identify $h(x)$ with the first height at which the capped component of $x$ contains a smaller vertex. Uniformly in intervals of length $L$, outside $O(L^{1-\\delta})$ starts, both clocks are $O(\\log L)$ at the actual least peak. This gives a purely atomic limit for $h(x)/x$ and an affine formula for inclusive cap densities. Counting sinks and cycles gives the reciprocal mean $5/6$ and the law of the minimum lost at a merger. In any interval of $L$ eligible heights $H$, the surviving minimum is at most $H L^{-1/100}$ at a limiting fraction $0.5726307930600027507\\ldots$ of heights, with a power-saving error. The sixfold cap has an exact right jump equal to the density of $h(x)=6x+2$. We compute the jump by an inverse ternary recursion. One arithmetic progression attains that height with shortest visitor clock ten, and other progressions require a logarithmic visitor clock. For each $0<\\epsilon<1$, every sufficiently large octave $[X,2X)$ contains at least $X^{1-\\epsilon}$ starts whose least peak equals their first-descent peak and exceeds $\\epsilon x\\log X/(9\\log3)$, despite a first descent in $O_\\epsilon(\\log\\log X)$ steps. The supplement replays finite checks using exact arithmetic.",
